52
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,570
How does logging drivers work?

53
00:04:19,133 --> 00:04:23,709
Where container output goes is decided by the logging driver.

54
00:04:23,709 --> 00:04:30,385
The default, json-file, writes one JSON line per log line into a file on the Docker host,

55
00:04:30,385 --> 00:04:33,685
without size limit unless you configure one:

56
00:04:33,685 --> 00:04:36,986
a chatty container can fill the host's disk.

57
00:04:36,986 --> 00:04:41,336
Other drivers keep logs in a compact local format (local),

58
00:04:41,336 --> 00:04:44,562
send them to the system journal (journald),

59
00:04:44,562 --> 00:04:51,763
to syslog, or to a central service (fluentd, gelf, awslogs, gcplogs, …), or discard them (none).

60
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,401
The engine's default driver:

61
00:04:55,401 --> 00:04:59,950
Each container records its driver and options in its configuration:

62
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,288
Each container records its driver and options in its configuration:

63
00:05:05,288 --> 00:05:10,219
chatty writes 50,000 lines of about 110 characters as fast as it can.

64
00:05:10,219 --> 00:05:13,864
Without rotation, all of them stay in its log file,

65
00:05:13,864 --> 00:05:21,296
and a long-running chatty service only ever adds to it (the file lives in the Docker host's file system,

66
00:05:21,296 --> 00:05:23,654
inside the VM on Docker Desktop):

67
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:29,328
