85
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:47,403
Why would I need buildKit and buildx?

86
00:06:47,967 --> 00:06:52,008
Since Docker 23, every docker build runs on BuildKit,

87
00:06:52,008 --> 00:06:59,708
the build engine, through the buildx client plugin (docker build is an alias of docker buildx build).

88
00:06:59,708 --> 00:07:04,664
BuildKit is what makes the features of the last lessons possible:

89
00:07:04,664 --> 00:07:09,391
parallel stages, cache mounts and secret mounts (RUN --mount),

90
00:07:09,391 --> 00:07:14,575
multi-platform builds (lesson 104), and outputs other than an image:

91
00:07:14,575 --> 00:07:20,903
files on your disk, a tar archive, an OCI layout, or a push straight to a registry.

92
00:07:20,903 --> 00:07:23,114
buildx also manages builders:

93
00:07:23,114 --> 00:07:30,891
the engine's own, or separate BuildKit instances in a container, on another machine, or in Kubernetes.

